## 1️⃣ Basic **HTTP‑Security** Headers

Add **helmet** to set sane defaults (X‑Frame‑Options, XSS‑Protection, HSTS, etc.):

```js
// app.js
const express = require('express');
const helmet  = require('helmet');          // <‑‑ add
const cors    = require('cors');            // <‑‑ optional but recommended
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const app = express();

app.use(helmet());           // All HTTP‑security headers
app.use(cors());             // Configure as needed (see below)
app.use(express.json());    // Body parser for JSON

// Example rate‑limit: 100 req/min per IP
const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 1 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 100,
  standardHeaders: true,    // Return rate‑limit in X‑RateLimit-* headers
  legacyHeaders: false
});
app.use(limiter);
```

> **Why?**  
> Helmet is a well‑maintained middleware that hardens your app against XSS, clickjacking, MIME sniffing, and other HTTP attacks.  
> CORS should *only* expose the routes you intend to be public. By default, we use the permissive config, but you can tighten it.

---

## 2️⃣ Verify That Only Expected Routes Are Exposed

Express will gladly return `404` for unknown routes, but adding an explicit *“catch‑all”* keeps the intent crystal clear and allows you to log the attempt.

```js
// At the end of the file, before starting to listen
app.all('*', (req, res) => {
  res.status(404).json({
    error: "Not Found",
    path: req.originalUrl,
    method: req.method
  });
});
```

> Logging the attempted path can help you spot malicious probing.

---

## 3️⃣ Route‑level Input Validation

Even though the current `/users` endpoint never processes external data, any real‑world endpoint that receives query or body parameters must validate them.  
Use `express-validator`, `joi`, or a lightweight manual check.

```js
const { query, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.get('/users',
  query('sort')
    .optional()
    .isIn(['name', 'id'])
    .withMessage('sort must be name or id'),

  (req, res, next) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      // Return 400 Bad Request with sanitized error messages
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }

    // YOUR (placeholder) business logic here
    const users = [
      { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email:'alice@example.com' },
      { id: 2, name: 'Bob',   email:'bob@example.com' }
    ];

    // If sorting requested, apply it (example only)
    const sorted = req.query.sort
      ? users.sort((a,b)=>a[req.query.sort].localeCompare(b[req.query.sort]))
      : users;

    res.json(sorted);
  }
);
```

> *Tip:* For production, keep the validation logic **outside** of route definitions by creating a `middleware/validators.js` module.

---

## 4️⃣ Central Error‑Handling Middleware

The simplest routes rarely throw, but if you add async logic or 3rd‑party libraries, you’ll need a global error handler that never leaks stack traces to clients.

```js
// after all route definitions, before app.listen:
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the error details for debugging
  console.error(`[${new Date().toISOString()}] ${err.stack ?? err.message}`);

  // Never expose stack traces or internal secrets
  res.status(err.statusCode || 500).json({
    error: err.message || 'Internal Server Error'
  });
});
```

> Express’ error handler takes **four** arguments, so make sure you pass `next` (even if unused).

