## 2️⃣  Centralised Error Handling

Express will bubble any thrown `Error` up to an *error‑handling middleware*.  Without one, the request will hang or the default (non‑secure) error output will leak stack traces to the browser.

```js
// errorHandler.js
module.exports = (err, req, res, next) => {
  // Log the error – use a proper logger (winston, pino, etc.)
  console.error(err);

  // Only show stack trace in development
  const isProd = process.env.NODE_ENV === 'production';
  const response = {
    message: err.message || 'Internal Server Error',
  };

  if (!isProd) {
    response.stack = err.stack;
  }

  res.status(err.status || 500).json(response);
};
```

And wire it into the app:

```js
// In app.js
const errorHandler = require('./errorHandler');

app.use(errorHandler);          // … *after* all routes
```

### Why it matters
- Prevents accidental leaks of stack trace data in production.
- Gives you a single place to log, email, or alert on critical errors.
- Allows you to convert an arbitrary `Error` into a proper HTTP status code.

